WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.960
- [Chef] It's cheesy,
comforting and indulgent.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.000
A truly great lasagna can turn

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.210
a bad day into a really good one.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:10.750
Here is our recipe for
the ultimate lasagna.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.200
(vibrant music)

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.930
When testing, we wanted to figure out

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.320
what was really worth spending our time on

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.610
and where we could take shortcuts

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:22.040
but still get the most incredible recipe.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.450
The sauce, this is definitely a place

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.560
where you wanna give it
the most time and love

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.930
because it is the star of the dish.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.010
A lot of recipes call for a bolognese,

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.400
which is a thick and creamy meat sauce

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.930
or a marinara, which is actually
just a simple tomato sauce.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.620
However, we found a hybrid

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.090
of the two gave us
something really delicious.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.780
For ours, we're using
a combination of meats:

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.510
half a pound of ground sirloin

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:48.550
because it's more tender and
flavorful than ground chuck,

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:52.090
half a pound of pork sausage
for complexity and savoriness,

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.950
and our secret, prosciutto.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.800
It adds so much flavor
and keeps the meat tender.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.010
We'll combine these in a bowl

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.320
and shape them into five or
six large shaggy meatballs.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.470
We're making them into meatballs

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.970
so they slowly break down
in the sauce over time,

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:08.540
essentially brazing it
to keep it really tender.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.090
Then add olive oil to a large
pot over medium-high heat.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.830
Once the oil is hot,
we'll brown our meatballs.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:16.950
This just takes about
a minute on each side.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.260
Make sure you do this in batches,

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.290
so you don't overcrowd the pan.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.190
Searing the meat locks in moisture,

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:23.510
and all those little brown bits

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:24.610
at the bottom of the pan will

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.830
actually give our sauce a ton of flavor.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.010
This is called the Maillard reaction.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.970
We'll set those aside, reduce the heat,

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:32.800
and then we'll add in our onions and salt.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.550
Let it cook until it's translucent.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:36.410
It only takes about five or seven minutes.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:38.820
Then we'll add in garlic, fresh thyme,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.890
fresh oregano, red pepper flakes,

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.960
and another one of our
secrets, crushed fennel seeds.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.260
These guys make the sauce
pop and offset its richness.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:48.630
I like using fresh herbs just

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.450
because I think they have more flavor,

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.380
and they're a lot more aromatic
than their dried variety.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.580
I like to crush the fennel
seeds with a mortar and pestle,

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.880
but if you don't have one,
a knife works great too.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.960
Saute these just until fragrant.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.750
Oh, my God, it is
smelling so good in here.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.720
I just love the smell of that garlic.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.860
The next thing we're gonna
do is add tomato paste.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.040
It thickens the sauce and
gives it a deep, rich flavor.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:10.680
Make sure to stir this frequently

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.080
until the color turns a dark red.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.470
This usually takes about
three to four minutes.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.840
This is about the color we want.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.590
Now it's time to deglaze our pan.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.060
We're using red wine, because
who doesn't love red wine?

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.190
But seriously, it has great flavor.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.270
You can also use beef stock
or even water if you prefer.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:27.960
You just need some type of liquid

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.900
to scrape up all those flavorful bits

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.060
on the bottom of the pan,

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.600
so they go into your sauce.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.720
Cook this until almost all of
the moisture is evaporated.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.320
In French cooking, this is called au sec.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:39.910
It's time to add our tomatoes.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.440
We're adding both whole San
Marzano tomatoes and passata,

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.490
which is also known as strained tomatoes.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.110
San Marzanos are grown in volcanic ash,

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:51.670
which makes them rich,
tender, and not overly acidic.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.730
Passata tomatoes are strained
of their seeds and skin,

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.840
making for a deliciously smooth sauce.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:58.930
We'll add these to the pot.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:01.130
And this next part might sound weird,

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:02.950
but we're actually gonna
add a whole carrot.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:05.560
The carrot will absorb
acidity from the tomatoes

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.110
and add sweetness to the sauce.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:08.810
But don't worry, we'll remove it later.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.470
Next, add butter.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.370
This is a secret from Italian
legend Marcella Hazan,

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:14.660
and we agree.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.400
You cannot skip it.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.160
It makes for a velvety rich
sauce that cannot be beat.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:21.990
And finally, add in your meatballs

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.530
and two teaspoons of salt.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.610
Bring this to a boil, then
cover and reduce to a simmer.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.000
Let it cook for about 1 1/2 to two hours.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.800
The longer it cooks, the
more flavor it will develop.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.240
Once the sauce has thickened,

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.380
discard that carrot
and use a potato masher

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.860
to crush those meatballs
into small pieces.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.520
You can season it more to taste here,

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.670
and if the sauce isn't
sweet enough for you,

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.070
just add a teaspoon of sugar.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:45.010
Then stir in fresh basil leaves.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:46.160
We like to use fresh

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.770
because it adds brightness
to the rich sauce.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.770
My grandmother always
told me to hand tear it.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:51.880
I have no idea why.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.220
But if you're in our family,
you do what Betty says.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.330
Now it's time to make the bechamel sauce.

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.990
We tested making the
lasagna with a ricotta,

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.610
a bechamel, and even a bechamel ricotta.

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:05.100
Ultimately, we liked the creamy
base of the bechamel best

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.640
because it kept the lasagna moist,

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.000
and it was super easy to
spread over the noodles.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.650
We'll start by melting our
butter until it starts to foam,

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:13.340
and then we'll add in our flour.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:15.250
Let's cook this until
we don't see any lumps

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.540
but the roux hasn't browned yet.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.450
This usually takes about three minutes.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.500
Now we're gonna slowly add in our milk

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.150
and keep whisking until
it's evenly incorporated.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:24.470
Then add in the salt,

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.970
garlic powder and nutmeg
and keep whisking.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.750
You'll know it's done
when it's thick enough

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.540
to coat the back of a
spoon just like this.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:32.810
Now we're gonna turn off the heat

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:34.560
so we don't burn our beautiful sauce

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.680
and stir in parmigiano-reggiano cheese.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.050
We like to grate this ourselves

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.150
because it melts down better in the sauce,

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:42.990
and it just has a more pronounced flavor

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.710
than if you buy the store-bought kind.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:45.910
The nutty sharp flavor

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.050
of the parmigiano-reggiano compliments

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.680
the creaminess of the
bechamel just really well.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.340
So our bechamel is complete,

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:54.740
and there's one last thing
to talk about, noodles.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.550
We tested regular store-bought,
oven-ready store-bought,

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.330
and even made them from scratch.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:00.780
The regular noodles ended up

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.270
just a little too mushy at the end.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.340
From scratch was very, very good

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.750
but we also found that
the texture just ended up

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.430
to be a little too soft,

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.580
and ultimately, it wasn't worth the time.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:13.360
Oven-ready was surprisingly our favorite.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.970
We loved it for its texture.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:16.890
It just had more of a bite to it.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.340
I know, we were shocked too.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:20.830
A little trick we like to do
is actually soak our noodles

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:22.810
in hot water and olive oil

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:24.320
for about five to 10 minutes

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.270
or just until it's softened a tiny bit,

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:27.880
where you can bend them just like this.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.020
This helps prevent them
from absorbing the sauce

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.080
when cooking and drying out your lasagna.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.020
Now, it's time to build
our beautiful baby.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.160
We'll start by greasing our baking dish.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.890
This lasagna is big. She's got layers.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:41.320
So your dish should be at
least three inches deep.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:42.780
Our first layer is meat sauce,

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.300
and this is gonna help the
noodles not stick to the pan.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.740
Next, we'll add our noodles.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.700
Over those, we'll spread out
an even layer of bechamel,

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:51.440
then we'll add a second
layer of meat sauce,

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.160
followed by fontina and mozzarella cheese.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.610
We love this combo because
the fontina's nutty

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.870
and sharp flavor contrasted really well

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:00.920
with the creaminess of the mozzarella.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:02.700
Next, we'll add another layer of noodles,

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.270
placing them in the opposite
direction as the first layer,

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:06.540
so there are no gaps.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:08.890
And finally, another layer of bechamel.

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:10.900
Repeat this to make four more layers,

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.580
ending with a layer of noodles.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.490
In my opinion, it's not really a lasagna

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:16.280
unless there's at least four layers.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:18.710
We'll top this off with
one more cup of bechamel

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:20.630
and dollop more meat sauce on top of that.

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:22.580
Scatter the remaining fontina on top,

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.600
then add fresh mozzarella cheese on top.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.660
We love fresh mozzarella
because it gets nice and brown,

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:29.960
and it's creamy, and I
don't know, I just like it.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:31.950
You can totally just
use shredded mozzarella.

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:33.880
I'm honestly just being extra.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.220
Finally add the grated Parmesan on top

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:37.450
and cover it with foil.

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:39.850
Make sure to coat your
foil with nonstick spray,

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:41.530
so the cheese doesn't stick to it.

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:43.140
And now, it's time to bake.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:44.910
We'll bake the lasagna for 30 minutes

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:46.390
and then remove the foil and bake it

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:48.030
for another 25 to 30

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:49.780
just until the cheese is bubbling,

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:51.660
and then let it cool for 20 minutes.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.090
We worked so hard, and now it
is time to reward ourselves.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.860
We promise you this lasagna will be a hit

00:06:57.860 --> 00:06:59.240
with your family and friends.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.350
Just whip it up on a cozy Sunday afternoon

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.580
and enjoy leftovers throughout the week.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:06.453
You can find this recipe
and many more on Tasty.co.

